window.docContents[114].resolve({"/ISO-80000-4-___-Mechanics/Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-ISO-80000-4/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-4-___-Mechanics--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-ISO-80000-4":{"contents":"\n\nA catalogued kind pairs a dimensioned quantity-kind with its\nexact source in the series — the part, the printed item designation (e.g. \"4-9.1\"), the\nprincipal quantity symbol, and the coherent SI unit symbol. The citation travels with\nthe kind as data, so the source of each definition can be rendered or audited downstream\nrather than kept in a comment.\n\nRealized as structure CataloguedKind over a DimensionedKind, with cite rendering\ne.g. ISO 80000-4, Second edition, 2019-08 item 4-9.1. The catalogue lists all 54\nitems in item order — mass (4-1), momentum (4-8), force (4-9.1), torque (4-12.2),\npressure (4-14.1), power (4-27), action (4-32), … — each paired with its coherent SI\nunit symbol.\n\nForce is M·L·T⁻² (item 4-9.1). The catalogued force kind carries time-exponent\nminus two in PhysLib's dimension group — the standard's dimensional statement reproduced\nas a checked computation, not an annotation. Uses the dimension map.\n\nProved as force_dim_time : force.dim.time = -2 in the Iso80000 library, discharged by\nnorm_num over the PhysLib dimension lemmas. The companions energy_dim_length = 2\n(item 4-28), pressure_dim_length = -1 (item 4-14.1), and power_dim_time = -3\n(item 4-27) record the rest of the mechanical algebra.\n\nA kilogram and a newton are not commensurable. Mass and force are distinct kinds, so\ntheir units cannot be compared by ratio — a type-level fact, not a runtime check. Uses\nmetrological units.\n\nProved as kilogram_newton_not_commensurable : ¬ kilogram.Commensurable newton by\nunfolding Commensurable to the underlying kinds and decide. The kilogram and the\nnewton are each WellFormed (mass and force are ratio-scale).\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009ISO 80000-4 — Mechanics","header":"23.1. Quantity-kinds and units of ISO 80000-4","id":"/ISO-80000-4-___-Mechanics/Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-ISO-80000-4/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-4-___-Mechanics--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-ISO-80000-4"},"/ISO-80000-9-___-Physical-chemistry-and-molecular-physics/The-mole-reduces-to-dimension-one-___-R13-at-its-sharpest/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-9-___-Physical-chemistry-and-molecular-physics--The-mole-reduces-to-dimension-one-___-R13-at-its-sharpest":{"contents":"ISO 80000-9 is built on the mole. In the scale-spanning reading (R13), the mole is not a\nbase dimension but a human-selected dimensionless count: N_A entities. So amount of\nsubstance is dimension one, and the /mol in every molar quantity is invisible to the\ndimension functor. Molar mass (kg/mol) carries the dimension of mass; molar volume\n(m³/mol) the dimension of volume; the amount-of-substance concentration (mol/m³) the\ndimension of a number density L⁻³ — the very same dimension as a particle concentration;\nmolality (mol/kg) the dimension of an inverse mass.\n\nAnd amount of substance itself joins the dimensionless family, distinct in kind from the\nnumber of entities it counts (N = n·N_A) — both dimension one, two kinds. This is\nrequirement R13 in the place that makes it unavoidable: a mol is no more a base unit\nthan a cd (ISO 80000-7) or a Sh (IEC 80000-13).\n\n\n\nMolar mass is a mass — the mole reduces (item 9-4). With the mole dimension one, kg/mol\ncarries the dimension of mass, M. The companion facts record the rest of the reduction:\nthe amount-of-substance concentration is a number density (equal to the particle\nconcentration), molality is an inverse mass, and amount of substance is itself dimension\none. This is the scale-spanning reduction (R13) at the place the mole lives. Uses\nthe dimension map and the scale-spanning\nunits.\n\nmolarMass_dim_eq_mass : molarMass.dim = Dim.mass, by reflexivity (the kind is declared at\nDim.mass). The companions amountOfSubstance_dim_eq_one,\namountConcentration_dim_eq_particleConcentration, and molality_dim_eq_inverseMass\nrecord the rest; the defining-relation versions\n(molarMass_dim_from_mass_amount, amountConcentration_dim_from_amount_volume) derive the\nreduction from M = m/n and c = n/V with dim n = 1. Axiom-free.\n\nThe dimension-1 disambiguation, on the standard — with the mole inside it. There exist\ndistinct ISO 80000-9 kinds with the same dimension one — amount of substance (the mole,\nreduced by R13) and the number of entities witness it, alongside the fractions, the\nactivities, and the partition functions. Dimension cannot separate them; the kind layer\ndoes. Uses the dimension map.\n\niso80000_9_dim_one_collision, witnessed by ⟨amountOfSubstance, numberOfEntities, …⟩\nwith the kind distinction a decide and reflexivity. Axiom-free.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009ISO 80000-9 — Physical chemistry and molecular physics","header":"28.2. The mole reduces to dimension one — R13 at its sharpest","id":"/ISO-80000-9-___-Physical-chemistry-and-molecular-physics/The-mole-reduces-to-dimension-one-___-R13-at-its-sharpest/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-9-___-Physical-chemistry-and-molecular-physics--The-mole-reduces-to-dimension-one-___-R13-at-its-sharpest"},"/Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework/The-trust-chain___-link-by-link/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework--The-trust-chain___-link-by-link":{"contents":"From a kinded source to bits on a device, each link is either a theorem or a named trusted base.\nEvery theorem cited here depends on propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound only;\nMatch.isBisimulation_contracted depends on propext and Quot.sound.\n\n* Link* Carried by* Trusted\n* Kinded source to the erased kernel* @[simp] erasure lemmas proved by rfl (Quantity.add_magnitude, …): the naked kernel is the kinded one with .magnitude applied* nothing\n* Erased kernel to the binary32 rungs* Quantity.add_refines at the rounding specification, unconditionally; Quantity.add_refines_exec at the executable binary32, under the explicit hypothesis that the sum is finite* nothing; overflow is a hypothesis, not an assumption\n* Kernel to its tape* the denotation bridge (TapeParity.Evaluates) and its homomorphism Evaluates.isNumCarrierHom, from TorchLean's per-operator Tape.apply correctness* nothing\n* Tape to compacted tape to printed program* pipeline_denotes: at every environment, the program printed from the compacted tape holds at the remapped slot of every original node the value the original tape computes there; SL.genSL_denotes for the program alone, table fetches included* nothing, up to the program; the printer's dozen forms meaning in the host language what they mean in Lean's Float\n* Tape graph to provenance hypergraph* Match.isWeakBisimulation_weak, and Match.isBisimulation_contracted on the contracted tape graph; the seal of the computation (TapeSeal.semantic_seal), carried to the printed program by semantic_seal_program* the harvest's reading of the source, which the bisimulation cross-checks rather than trusts\n* Printed program to device* for a table fetch, TorchLean's CUDA.denotes_emit: the generated CUDA source denotes the kernel expression Program.of certified against its Lean source* the host compiler with contraction and reassociation disabled; the host's IEEE conformance; exp and log from glibc, libdevice or Julia's Base.Math, checked by the parity executables to an ulp bound\n\n\n\nThe executable binary32 rung, with overflow stated. For kind-k quantities carried at\nthe executable binary32 (FloatLib's ExecFloat.Binary 8 23, on which TorchLean builds), if the executable sum is finite, forgetting it to ℝ gives the\nbinary32 rounding of the real sum. Where the hypothesis fails (an overflow to infinity, a NaN\noperand) the theorem claims nothing: it proves the equality on the finite path, not its failure off\nit, and the hypothesis marks that boundary.\n\nFloatLib's ExecFloat.Binary.toReal_add_eq_roundAt, transported along the kind index: the kinded sum is the\nnaked sum under Quantity.mk.\n\nThe denotation bridge is a homomorphism. The bridge relates both leaf formers and each of +,\n−, ×, ÷, min, max, sqrt and exp on the recording carrier to the matching\nelementwise operation on tensors, bundled as one record. The tape_hom tactic closes a model's\nparity by unfolding the model's own definitions and walking the exposed operation tree with\nthese cases.\n\nEach field is one of the per-operation preservation lemmas of the denotation bridge, which are\nproved from the tape's forward-value faithfulness lemmas through a run bridge.\n\nTape, compacted tape and program agree. For every well-formed tape without table fetches, if\nthe straight-line program is produced from its compacted form, then at every environment the\nprogram holds, at the remapped slot of every original node, exactly the value the original tape\ncomputes there. Constant leaves merged by compaction are covered, through the injectivity of\nFloat.toBits.\n\nThe compaction's denotation theorem, cseCompact_denotation_unconditional, composed with the\nprogram's denotation theorem for table-free tapes; the program's existence supplies the\ncompacted tape's evaluation.\n\nThe sharing recorder records the compacted tape. A carrier-generic model names a shared\nsub-expression once per use, so the recording carrier emits the expression tree and compaction\nshares it afterwards; the tree can be orders of magnitude larger than the model. The sharing\nrecorder looks each node up by cseCompact's key before appending it. For outputs related\noperation by operation at the two carriers and recorded from the empty tape at one tensor shape,\nboth recordings fail, or the sharing tape has the name, the operands and the stored value of\nevery node of the compacted unshared tape, in the same order, and its output ids are the\nremapped ids. Such a tape holds each key once (keys_distinct_of_fwd). That is the statement\nfor the unguarded entry recordRaw. The entry models use, record, also rejects a recording in\nwhich one input name holds two different tensors, which the unshared recorder accepts; for it\nthe statement is one-sided (shares_record_guarded): when record succeeds, the unshared\nrecording succeeds and the same agreement holds.\n\nA simulation between the sharing state and cseCompact's fold over the unshared tape, one node\nat a time (Rel.step), and a lemma that each elementwise recorder reads only its operands'\nvalues (raw_natural), which makes the lookup before running sound. The relation Shares is\npreserved by leaves, constants, + - * /, min, max, fmin, fmax, exp, log, abs,\nsqrt and the table fetch; the tactic tape_shares closes it for a concrete model.\n\nThe framework's own link. TorchLean's CUDA emitter: if emission of a kernel expression\nsucceeds, the generated source denotes the expression for any arithmetic and for buffer\ncontents read through bounds-checked loads. Program.of pairs each expression with a proof that\nit agrees with the Lean function it was recognized from. Compilation, memory and execution\nare outside the statement.\n\nA theorem of TorchLean, linked here because the table fetch's device kernel rests on it.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Metrology on a Verified Numerical Framework","header":"19.2. The trust chain, link by link","id":"/Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework/The-trust-chain___-link-by-link/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework--The-trust-chain___-link-by-link"}});